advection, capillary diffusion and gravitational flow two-phase, takes into account element shape in 3D

.PP
Method computes the minimum CFL criterion for the current model on the basis of the volumetric troughput through each finite volume cell\&. This is done taking into account that the velocity of the non-wetting phase differs from the total velocity and that the inflow into a cell is not necessarily equivalent to the outflow (as in the case of transient flows or nodalsources or sinks)\&.

.PP
The calculations are performed for the supplied relative permeability model\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The method needs a reference to the current model in order to determine the flow velocity distribution\&.

.PP
The specific relative permeability model that shall be used in the computations is returned as second method argument\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The method returns the minimum global timestep for the current mesh and velocity distribution for which the the transport distance in each element is smaller or equal to its diameter\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The criteria are evaluated on an element by element basis using the FV sector volumes to find CFL\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
THIS IS RECOMMENDED FOR USE (1D VERSION) for an explicit transport calculation\&. It checks everything: advection, diffusion and gravity-driven flow\&.

.PP
Overload this method if you use a less restrictive transport scheme than IMPES is\&. Computes the Courant time increment (CFL citerion) taking into account viscous, gravitational and capillary fluid displacements using the contraints from the provided relative permeability model\&. The CFL criterion is calculated using the element diameter in the direction of the flow but not account for the deviation from this \fBvector<double>\fP of the flow of the considered phase\&.

.PP
For the viscous flow the shock speed is used as a multiplier for the total velocity\&. This may lead to a too tight constraint but is safe for the case where CFL is computed only once at the onset of series of advection steps with an explicit scheme\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
First, a const reference to the current model, a reference to the \fBTwoPhaseModel\fP used (this will be a subclass), and a maximum time-increment imposed by external constraints\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The CFL criterion\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The criterion is computed sector by sector for all the finite elements in the entire model\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Method has been designed primarily to give stability to an explicit transport scheme but it also serves as a guide for the timestepping using an implicit approach\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
The method alerts the user to the case where CFL is larger than the input max_time_increment which may be the case if there is no flow at all in the domain\&. Equally, the user is informed if the CFL increment is less than a millisecond (usually a prohibitively small increment)\&. 
.PP
Reimplemented in \fBcsmp::TwoPhaseExplicitNodeCenteredFVTransport< dim, STP >\fP, \fBcsmp::TwoPhaseImplicitNodeCenteredFVTransport< dim, STP >\fP, and \fBcsmp::TwoPhaseImplicitNodeCenteredFVTransport< dim, csmp::StencilProcessor >\fP\&.
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::NodeCenteredFiniteVolumeTransport\fP< dim >::AssignFluxBoundaryConditions (\fBNodeCenteredFiniteVolumeAlgorithm\fP< dim > & advection_algorithm, bool use_t0_saturation) const\fR [protected]\fP"
Compensates for inflow and outflow at model boundaries where the finite volumes are truncated\&.

.PP
NB: This method does not compensate for diffusive fluxes which require the explicit specification of boundary conditions by the user\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
Apart from references to the instances of the current model and transport algorithm, this method allows the user to specify whether the saturation from the first time level shall be used (this is required if second-order accuracy in time is desired) and whether it is permitted to treat in and outfluxes implicitly\&. While this is the preferred option, it may lead to a negative matrix diagonal\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
Method uses \fBFluxThroughBoundaryFiniteVolume()\fP to evaluate in and outfluxes of the domain\&. This implies that only 1st-order fluxes will be considered since because the transport variable is not interpolated to any of the facets of the halo stencil\&.

.PP
A rationale for this comes from the frequent situation that the transported variable is not expected to be continuous across the domain boundary\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
This method is used in implicit advection calculations\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::NodeCenteredFiniteVolumeTransport\fP< dim >::AssignScalarBoundaryValues (\fBBOX_BOUNDARY\fP boundary, const char * property, \fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP bcond, double val, bool distribute_total_amount)"

.PP
assignment of a single specific value that is projected on outward point normal For box-shaped models, this method allows to assign boundary normal surface integrated variables using the finite volume discretization\&. The method works only for box-shaped models because otherwise it would need to have calculations of the surface normal which it can't do without access to face variables\&.

.PP
This could for instance be a surface normal flux that shall be integrated over the surface area of the finite elements\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Use method to integrate variables like "nodal volume source"\&. Resulting quantities can be accumulated directly (without integration) into the finite element equations\&. For this purpose, PDE operators like the \fBPointSource_rhsop\fP can be used\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::NodeCenteredFiniteVolumeTransport\fP< dim >::BackupFacetFluxes ()\fR [protected]\fP"
The facet fluxes from the first time-level are backuped into the \fBvector<double>\fP FACETFLUXES0 so that they can used in the next calculation to obtain second-order accuracy in time for the transport scheme\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> double \fBcsmp::NodeCenteredFiniteVolumeTransport\fP< dim >::BoundaryFluxes (double & in_flow, double & out_flow, bool box_shaped = \fRtrue\fP, bool use_advected_variable = \fRfalse\fP, const char * advected_variable = \fR'no advected variable'\fP, const char * variable_to_determine_no_flow = \fR'fluid pressure'\fP, \fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP exclude_flag = \fR\fBANY\fP\fP) const"

